    "C74","C76","C77","C79","C80","C81","C82","C83","C84"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is ginsenoside Rb1, a tetraglycosylated PPD-type dammarane saponin. The closest supplied context, ginsenoside Rg3, has low oral bioavailability in humans and 2.63% in rats at 10 mg/kg.",
      "card_ids": ["C21","C26","C34"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Other triterpene saponins are consistently low or poor after oral dosing: glycyrrhizin is extremely low and barely detectable in plasma, while astragaloside IV has oral bioavailability of 7.4% in dogs and 3.66% in rats.",
      "card_ids": ["C04","C10","C11","C17","C29","C30","C06","C28","C36"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 permeability and absorption records show very low intestinal permeability for ginsenosides and related glycosides: Rh2 Papp about 1.9×10^-8 cm/s, NGR1 about 1×10^-8 cm/s, Rg1-containing PAMPA Pe 8.61×10^-8 cm/s, stevioside Papp 0.16×10^-6 cm/s, Rh2s colon absorption below 10%, and astragaloside IV GI absorption 2.2–3%.",
      "card_ids": ["C49","C50","C51","C52","C53","C54","C55","C56","C57","C58","C59"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 bidirectional transport records show highly glycosylated ginsenosides including Rb1-type compounds have Papp below 0.3×10^-6 cm/s with efflux ratio near unity; Rb1 and Rg1 have Papp of 10^-7–10^-8 cm/s; Rh2 has extremely low permeability and is classified as BCS class IV.",
      "card_ids": ["C61","C62","C63","C66","C67","C68","C70","C71"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 microbiota records show PPD-type ginsenosides including Rb1 are deglycosylated by intestinal β-glucosidase into Rd, F2, Rg3, compound K, Rh2, or PPD, and ginsenoside Rc is deglycosylated by human fecal microflora, reducing intact parent absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C64","C69","C72"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records show measurable intrinsic clearance for related ginsenosides and aglycones in human hepatic microsomes or recombinant CYP3A4 (Rh1 4.8, Rg2 2.1, Rf 2.1/1.3, PPT 6.9, PPD 35 µL/min/mg) and MRP2-mediated transport of Rg1 (2.03 µL/min/mg), supporting hepatic and transport-mediated elimination as an additional contributor to low systemic exposure; no Rb1-specific hepatic-stability record is present.",
      "card_ids": ["C74","C76","C77","C79","C80","C81","C82","C83","C84"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C73 and C78 concern phenolic glycosides (verproside, catalposide) and UGT/SULT pathways that are not transferable to the non-phenolic dammarane saponin query.",
      "card_ids": ["C73","C78"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C73","C74","C75","C76","C77","C78","C79","C80","C81","C82","C83","C84"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct measured absolute oral bioavailability value is available for the query molecule itself; the decision relies on transfer from ginsenoside Rg3 and other triterpene saponins.",
    "L6 hepatic-clearance values are for related ginsenosides and aglycones (Rh1, Rg2, Rf, PPT, PPD, Rg1), not for Rb1 itself; no Rb1-specific hepatic microsome or CYP data are present.",
    "L4/L5 records are in vitro permeability and microbiota metabolism assays, not direct systemic F measurements; translation to human F is indirect.",
    "No formulation, dose, or prandial-state data are available for the query; absorption enhancers could improve permeability, but even enhanced Rh2 Papp values remain low.",
    "Gut-microbiota deglycosylation may generate absorbable metabolites such as compound K, but this does not support intact parent F at or above 20%.",
    "C73 and C78 involve non-ginsenoside phenolic glycosides and are not transferable to the non-phenolic dammarane saponin query."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "The query is ginsenoside Rb1, a tetraglycosylated PPD-type dammarane saponin. The closest transferable context, ginsenoside Rg3, has low oral bioavailability in humans and 2.63% in rats; other triterpene saponins such as glycyrrhizin and astragaloside IV are also low or poor. L4 permeability records show very low intestinal permeability for ginsenosides, and L5 records show highly glycosylated ginsenosides including Rb1-type compounds have Papp below 0.3×10^-6 cm/s with efflux ratio near unity, Rh2 is BCS class IV, and PPD-type ginsenosides are deglycosylated by intestinal microbiota. L6 hepatic-clearance records add mechanistic support for hepatic and transport-mediated elimination of related ginsenosides, although no Rb1-specific hepatic-stability record is present. No affirmative evidence supports F at or above 20%; the prediction remains low and is strengthened."
}

